15
00:01:55.410 --> 00:02:06.980
Potuz: Oh, yeah, so actually, let's try to go about this the other way around. I didn't even know about this, and I'm just looking at this, and it looks like the VC asking directly to… contacting directly to builders.

16
00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:07.970
Potuz: Oh.

17
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:08.410
Luca | Vero: that.

18
00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:23.079
Potuz: Which my first impression is that this is absolutely crazy. So instead of like, yeah, I would have liked to know about this before. I know it's my mistake. I'm pretty sure that most people don't know about this.

19
00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:38.039
Luca | Vero: I have been, trying to raise awareness of this. It's been open for 2 months, and I've been mentioning it on these calls and on the Discord. But, okay, so let me just give a quick intro. The idea is indeed that the validator client would

20
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:46.840
Luca | Vero: contact builders, and then it would pick a bid, and it would supply a bid to the connected Beacon nodes, and those Beacon nodes would then

21
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.909
Luca | Vero: Use that bit in their block that they're producing.

22
00:02:52.450 --> 00:03:01.340
Potuz: Yeah, just like two days, one hour, a few hours before a fork in Sepolia and a few weeks before mainnet fork.

23
00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.770
Potuz: This seems like a huge change for me.

24
00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.359
Luca | Vero: Yeah, I don't think anybody thinks, well.

25
00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:19.079
Luca | Vero: It doesn't seem that necessary for Sepolia, that's why I haven't pushed for it earlier.

26
00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:22.990
Luca | Vero: I've always imagined it would be useful starting with Hoodi

27
00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:33.609
Luca | Vero: I have been testing this endpoint for a few weeks on DevNet 8 as well.

28
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:38.540
Enrico Del Fante: Can you go back to the reason why we want to do this?

29
00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.119
Enrico Del Fante: Seems like your question, your first question you asked.

30
00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:50.379
Enrico Del Fante: The answer should be yes, because I don't get why it's required at the moment.

31
00:03:52.400 --> 00:04:00.209
Luca | Vero: I think it's useful when you have a validator client connected to different CL clients, like, even 5 or 6 different ones.

32
00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:10.090
Luca | Vero: That way, you don't have to monitor all of those CL clients metrics, you just need to monitor your validator client.

33
00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:15.079
Luca | Vero: Makes these node operations easier when you have multiple clients involved.

34
00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:25.069
Enrico Del Fante: So it seems like the VC should be in charge of selecting

35
00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:40.459
Enrico Del Fante: the bin, because this is where the logic of selecting the most valuable thing resides. So, when you have multiple nodes, the VC wants to know about all the bids around and select the one that it likes.

36
00:04:40.849 --> 00:04:46.799
Enrico Del Fante: And submit that one, along with the block production request? Is that the reason?

37
00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.720
Luca | Vero: Yeah, that's basically how it would work.

38
00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:51.890
Luca | Vero: But why can't you have just one?

39
00:04:51.890 --> 00:05:03.900
Potuz: one Beacon node connects. So, I mean, today the Beacon is the one that requires. Why don't you just make one of the nodes contact the builder on all of the other nodes to not do that?

40
00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:10.510
Luca | Vero: Well, because if that one node goes offline, then you're losing access to bids.

41
00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:16.770
Potuz: And isn't it the same if the one VC goes offline? I'm a little bit confused.

42
00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.819
Potuz: I mean, you have one endpoint that calls.

43
00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:26.960
Luca | Vero: Yeah, but it's different, because you can have a failover for the VC.

44
00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.000
Luca | Vero: or.

45
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.220
Potuz: Why can't you have a failover for the Beacon node then?

46
00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.630
Luca | Vero: You can, but then you have to…

47
00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.579
Luca | Vero: You do still have multiple Beacon nodes involved.

48
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.359
Luca | Vero: But, like I said, you would need to track

49
00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.309
Luca | Vero: How all of those beacon notes are doing this.

50
00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:51.730
Luca | Vero: And all of those beacon notes would basically be contacting the same builders.

51
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.519
Luca | Vero: With the same request.

52
00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.190
Luca | Vero: So it just seems…

53
00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:57.510
Potuz: I don't know.

54
00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.770
Luca | Vero: I'm sorry.

55
00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:02.110
Potuz: You want to avoid many calls to the Beacon node.

56
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:13.980
Potuz: To the… to the builders. Which, if you just put it… if you put them on only one Beacon node, you achieve this. But then you say, okay, no, we cannot… we may not do this because that

57
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:26.130
Potuz: color might fail, which is the case for the VC. If you have a single color in the VC, that color might fail, and you say, okay, you can have a failover, but you can do the same with a single beacon node.

58
00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:45.349
Potuz: You would just say, one of the nodes is going to be this node that is a prism, and if that one fails, you fail to get bids, but okay, so then let's put another prism that is a failover for that prism, and it only acts if that other prism is offline, and then now you only get called once. I don't understand why

59
00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:49.619
Potuz: Why this cannot be achieved on the Beacon node and yes on the VC?

60
00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.700
Luca | Vero: I'm not sure what you're proposing here in terms of failover Beacon node.

61
00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:10.669
Potuz: just the usual thing, that you have a Beko node, and if that Beko node is offline, then another Beko node takes its place, which I assume

62
00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.979
Potuz: You are telling me that you have to do anyways this on the VC side.

63
00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:28.599
Luca | Vero: Yeah, well, multi-node validator clients usually work with all of the Beacon nodes they're connected to, so they don't work in the failover way.

64
00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:31.809
Luca | Vero: They would ask all of the connected Beaconos to.

65
00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:42.499
Potuz: But if you don't have a failover for the validator client, then you still have a single point of failure to the calls for the bits.

66
00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:51.519
Potuz: Which you would have, therefore, the same single point of failure if you just put one Beacon node to call for the bits.

67
00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:57.939
Luca | Vero: It's better if you have the failover validator client.

68
00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:05.079
Luca | Vero: Because it's easier to failover in that case.

69
00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:15.640
Luca | Vero: You can… if you have a shared splashing protection database among multiple different validated clients, you can, in theory, have them running at the same time.

70
00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:23.140
Luca | Vero: But I don't want to get into these details, I should say.

71
00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.270
Luca | Vero: Assuming there is a reason.

72
00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.989
Luca | Vero: This endpoint is beneficial.

73
00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:32.910
