89
00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:13.199
Nico Flaig: So mine are really technical, I guess. So the first thing, like, just from an implementation point of view, is that Vero needs the payload attributes to know what bits to request from the builder.

90
00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:28.629
Nico Flaig: And we currently emit them in the previous slot based on, like, a prediction, right? So it can happen that Vero gets a bid from the builders that are not even compliant with our proposal head, which we call at the start of the slot.

91
00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:41.670
Nico Flaig: That is one thing. The other problem I see, and this is also implementation detail that could be optimized, is Vero will call the ProduceBlockV4 API later on the slot.

92
00:10:41.970 --> 00:11:03.390
Nico Flaig: Because it does it after playing the timing game with the builders, and users will definitely play timing games at some point, I would expect. So, it calls the API late in the slot, and I'm just not sure, what is the implementation, like, the problem here, because right now, on the BeaconNode side, we just assume this is called at the start of the slot.

93
00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:13.919
Nico Flaig: At least in Loadstar, we are not optimizing, this to be caught late on the slot yet, but it could be done, it's just an implementation thing.

94
00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:25.510
Nico Flaig: And then I guess the third concern is more implementation, which is you… Basically, at, I think, 2 or 3 branches in the Broduse Block API.

95
00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:33.719
Nico Flaig: I don't think it's a big deal, that one. I mean, I have it implemented, but… Just something to consider.

96
00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:41.010
